1
00:00.500 --> 00:03.100 align:start
Welcome to the glaze room here at Kestrel Clay Studio.

2
00:03.100 --> 00:06.100
Before you dip a single pot,

3
00:06.100 --> 00:09.500
remember that everything on these shelves is formulated for a

4
00:09.500 --> 00:12.300
<b>cone&nbsp;6</b> stoneware firing, which reaches about 1222&#176;C in our big

5
00:12.300 --> 00:15.500 align:end size:70%
electric kiln.

6
00:20.500 --> 00:23.100
Every piece you glaze today has already been bisque fired

7
00:23.100 --> 00:26.100
to <b>cone&nbsp;04</b>, so it is hard but still porous,

8
00:26.100 --> 00:29.500
and that porosity is what lets the raw glaze grab

9
00:29.500 --> 00:32.300 align:start
and dry onto the wall of the pot in seconds.

10
00:37.300 --> 00:40.500
We have three buckets open on the bench.

11
00:40.500 --> 00:43.100
The first is a classic <i>shino</i>,

12
00:43.100 --> 00:46.100
which breaks a warm toasty orange where it runs thin

13
00:46.100 --> 00:49.500 align:end size:70%
over an edge. The second is a deep glossy <i>tenmoku</i>

14
00:49.500 --> 00:52.300
that pools almost black and thins to a rich rust

15
00:52.300 --> 00:55.500
brown. The third is a soft milky <i>nuka</i> that we

16
00:55.500 --> 00:58.100
love over dark clay bodies.

17
01:03.100 --> 01:06.100 align:start
Application is mostly about thickness and timing.

18
01:06.100 --> 01:09.500
For most of these glazes you want a coat about

19
01:09.500 --> 01:12.300
as thick as a <i>credit&nbsp;card</i>.

20
01:12.300 --> 01:15.500
On our buckets that means a slow three second dip,

21
01:15.500 --> 01:18.100 align:end size:70%
a steady count, and then lift the pot straight out

22
01:18.100 --> 01:21.100
and let it drip back into the bucket.

23
01:26.100 --> 01:29.500
Before you dip, wax the foot ring and the bottom

24
01:29.500 --> 01:32.300
of every pot with our blue resist.

25
01:32.300 --> 01:35.500 align:start
Wax makes the glaze bead up and run right off

26
01:35.500 --> 01:38.100
the foot, which keeps molten glaze from gluing your pot

27
01:38.100 --> 01:41.100
to the kiln shelf and ruining both.

28
01:46.100 --> 01:49.500
Speaking of shelves, we brush a thin layer of kiln

29
01:49.500 --> 01:52.300 align:end size:70%
wash on every shelf for exactly the same reason.

30
01:52.300 --> 01:55.500
If a drip does land on a washed shelf,

31
01:55.500 --> 01:58.100
it chips off cleanly instead of welding itself to the

32
01:58.100 --> 02:01.100
surface forever.

33
02:06.100 --> 02:09.500 align:start
The defect you will fight most as a beginner is

34
02:09.500 --> 02:12.300
called <u>crawling</u>, where the glaze pulls back into little islands

35
02:12.300 --> 02:15.500
and leaves bare clay showing through.

36
02:15.500 --> 02:18.100
Crawling almost always comes from dust,

37
02:18.100 --> 02:21.100 align:end size:70%
oil, or a fingerprint left on the bisque,

38
02:21.100 --> 02:24.500
so handle clean pots with clean dry hands.

39
02:29.500 --> 02:32.300
If you are curious what is actually in the bucket,

40
02:32.300 --> 02:35.500
most of these recipes are built from the same three

41
02:35.500 --> 02:38.100 align:start
families of ingredient: silica for the glass,

42
02:38.100 --> 02:41.100
a feldspar to help it melt,

43
02:41.100 --> 02:44.500
and whiting to stiffen the melt and adjust the surface.

44
02:49.500 --> 02:52.300
Wipe the foot one last time with a damp sponge,

45
02:52.300 --> 02:55.500 align:end size:70%
load your pots so nothing touches its neighbor,

46
02:55.500 --> 02:58.100
and we will unload this glaze firing together on Thursday

47
02:58.100 --> 03:01.100
afternoon.
